(window.DOM_TESTS=window.DOM_TESTS||{})["lecture-7"]={"lecture":"lecture-7","chapters":["ecg"],"questions":[{"id":"lt-lecture-7:645cd64c","chapter":"ecg","section":"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record","type":"mechanism","stem":"An ECG electrode detects a voltage on the skin even though the impulse originates in the myocardium. What makes this possible?","options":{"A":"The heart mechanically transmits its pulse to every skin electrode.","B":"Cardiac electrical currents spread through conducting body tissues.","C":"The electrodes measure changes in circulating blood pressure.","D":"The chest electrodes directly contact ventricular muscle fibres."},"answer":"B","why":"Depolarisation and repolarisation create voltage differences that propagate through the conductive tissues of the body and can be detected at the skin.","not":{"A":"Mechanical pulsation is distinct from electrical potential recorded by ECG electrodes.","C":"Surface ECG electrodes do not directly measure intravascular blood pressure.","D":"The electrodes sit on intact skin, not within the myocardium."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:a388c25e","chapter":"ecg","section":"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record","type":"application","stem":"Two beats have different QRS heights. A student concludes the taller complex produced a larger stroke volume. What is the main flaw?","options":{"A":"QRS height represents the duration of ventricular contraction.","B":"QRS height directly records the pressure within the ventricle.","C":"QRS height measures the amount of venous blood returning.","D":"QRS height reflects voltage, not the amount of blood ejected."},"answer":"D","why":"The ECG measures electrical potentials. A larger recorded deflection may reflect electrical orientation or amplitude; it does not directly quantify mechanical stroke volume.","not":{"A":"Duration is measured horizontally on ECG paper, not from complex height.","B":"A surface ECG records electrical potential rather than ventricular pressure.","C":"Venous return is a haemodynamic variable not directly measured by the tracing."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:0ead4263","chapter":"ecg","section":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"A ventricular depolarisation vector runs almost perpendicular to the viewing axis of a lead. What should that lead record?","options":{"A":"A small or biphasic ventricular deflection.","B":"A tall upright ventricular deflection.","C":"A tall downward ventricular deflection.","D":"A prolonged ventricular depolarisation interval."},"answer":"A","why":"Only the component of the electrical vector along the lead axis contributes strongly to the deflection; a near-perpendicular vector has a small projection.","not":{"B":"A strongly upright deflection needs a substantial vector component towards the positive pole.","C":"A strongly downward deflection needs a substantial vector component away from the positive pole.","D":"Vector orientation changes amplitude and polarity, not necessarily conduction duration."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:a0437b54","chapter":"ecg","section":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","type":"application","stem":"Lead II has an upright QRS while aVR has a downward QRS during the same beat. What best explains the difference?","options":{"A":"The two leads record different mechanical contractions.","B":"Atrial and ventricular activity alternate between the leads on each beat.","C":"Each lead projects the same electrical vector onto a different axis.","D":"One lead measures depolarisation and the other repolarisation."},"answer":"C","why":"A lead is a directional electrical view. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector points generally towards Lead II and away from aVR.","not":{"A":"Both leads sample the electrical activity of the same heartbeat.","B":"Lead orientation does not change the sequence of atrial and ventricular activation.","D":"Both QRS complexes represent ventricular depolarisation, despite opposite polarity."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:8b83f998","chapter":"ecg","section":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","type":"mechanism","stem":"In a lead where the QRS is upright, the T wave is often upright too. Which combined explanation best accounts for this?","options":{"A":"Repolarisation has the same electrical sign and travels in the same direction through the ventricles.","B":"Repolarisation has opposite polarity and generally travels in the reverse direction.","C":"Repolarisation has opposite polarity but travels in the same direction.","D":"Repolarisation has the same polarity but travels in the reverse direction."},"answer":"B","why":"A repolarisation wave has the opposite electrical sign to depolarisation; ventricular repolarisation also progresses in the reverse order, producing a T wave often concordant with QRS.","not":{"A":"Depolarisation and repolarisation do not have the same electrical polarity.","C":"Opposite polarity without reversed direction would favour opposite QRS/T deflections.","D":"Reversed direction without the polarity reversal would also favour opposite deflections."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:ac400f51","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"application","stem":"A standard ECG is set up with arm, leg and chest electrodes, but the right-leg contact is omitted. Which function is most directly compromised?","options":{"A":"Recording the positive pole of Lead II.","B":"Positioning the negative pole of Lead III.","C":"Generating the six precordial lead views.","D":"Reducing unwanted electrical interference."},"answer":"D","why":"The standard right-leg electrode serves as a reference/grounding contact for noise reduction; it is not one of the positive viewing electrodes.","not":{"A":"Lead II uses the left leg as its positive pole.","B":"Lead III uses the left arm as its negative pole.","C":"The six precordial recording contacts are placed on the chest."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:f9b15762","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"recall","stem":"Which electrode polarity defines standard limb Lead I?","options":{"A":"Right arm negative; left arm positive.","B":"Left arm negative; left leg positive.","C":"Right arm negative; left leg positive.","D":"Left leg negative; right arm positive."},"answer":"A","why":"Standard limb Lead I measures the voltage difference from right arm (negative) to left arm (positive), looking approximately towards the patient’s left.","not":{"B":"That configuration defines standard limb Lead III.","C":"That configuration defines standard limb Lead II.","D":"Lead I does not use the left leg as its negative pole."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:378359e4","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"calculation","stem":"At one instant, Lead I records +0.4 mV and Lead III records −0.2 mV. What must Lead II record?","options":{"A":"−0.6 mV, from subtracting the two values.","B":"+0.6 mV, from adding their magnitudes.","C":"−0.2 mV, from using the third lead alone.","D":"+0.2 mV, from adding the signed voltages."},"answer":"D","why":"Einthoven’s law is V(II) = V(I) + V(III). Therefore +0.4 + (−0.2) = +0.2 mV at the same instant.","not":{"A":"This reverses the sign of the result rather than adding the signed voltages.","B":"The negative sign of Lead III must be retained in the sum.","C":"Lead II is obtained by combining the two measured limb-lead voltages."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:183d5f85","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why is aVF classed as an augmented unipolar limb lead?","options":{"A":"It compares two fixed chest electrodes in the horizontal plane of the heart.","B":"It uses the left-foot electrode against an averaged limb reference.","C":"It compares the right arm directly against the left arm.","D":"It uses the right-leg electrode as its positive terminal."},"answer":"B","why":"In aVF the left-foot electrode is positive and is compared with an average reference derived from the two arm electrodes.","not":{"A":"Chest electrodes make up V1–V6 and mainly examine the horizontal plane.","C":"Right arm versus left arm describes bipolar Lead I.","D":"The right-leg electrode is used for electrical noise reduction."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:77180045","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"What extra perspective do the chest leads V1–V6 add to the standard limb-lead views?","options":{"A":"They replace voltage measurement with intracardiac pressure.","B":"They isolate atrial electrical activity from ventricular activity.","C":"They show cardiac electrical vectors in the horizontal plane.","D":"They remove the need to record the frontal electrical plane."},"answer":"C","why":"The limb leads provide frontal-plane views, while the precordial leads examine the same heart from different points around the horizontal plane.","not":{"A":"Chest leads, like limb leads, still record surface electrical potentials.","B":"Both sets of leads record combined cardiac electrical events.","D":"Frontal and horizontal views complement rather than replace one another."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:2aea86b7","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"application","stem":"A technician places V1 at the fourth intercostal space. Where should the electrode be positioned horizontally?","options":{"A":"At the right sternal border.","B":"At the left midclavicular line.","C":"At the anterior axillary line.","D":"At the left midaxillary line."},"answer":"A","why":"V1 is positioned in the fourth intercostal space at the right edge of the sternum; V2 lies at the left sternal edge at the same level.","not":{"B":"The left midclavicular line in the fifth space is the usual site of V4.","C":"The anterior axillary position is used for V5.","D":"The midaxillary position is used for V6."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:7065b23f","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"application","stem":"V2 has been placed correctly and V4 is at its usual site. Where should V3 be attached?","options":{"A":"At the fourth space, right sternal border.","B":"At the fifth space, anterior axillary line.","C":"At the fifth space, midaxillary line.","D":"Halfway between the V2 and V4 electrodes."},"answer":"D","why":"V3 is placed between the already positioned V2 and V4, so the anatomical line between those contacts determines its location.","not":{"A":"The fourth right parasternal position belongs to V1.","B":"The anterior axillary line at V4 level is used for V5.","C":"The midaxillary line at V4 level is used for V6."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:ef8da0aa","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"application","stem":"Which positioning instruction for V5 and V6 is correct?","options":{"A":"Both sit one intercostal space above V4.","B":"V5 is anterior axillary; V6 is midaxillary, both level with V4.","C":"V5 is midaxillary; V6 is anterior axillary, both level with V4.","D":"Both sit in the fourth intercostal space beside the sternum."},"answer":"B","why":"V5 is attached on the anterior axillary line, and V6 on the midaxillary line, both at the horizontal level of V4.","not":{"A":"V5 and V6 should remain level with V4, not a space above it.","C":"This exchanges the normal anterior and midaxillary placements.","D":"Fourth-space parasternal contacts are V1 and V2."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:b8e00307","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"mechanism","stem":"A standard ECG uses ten skin electrodes but produces twelve electrical views. How is this possible?","options":{"A":"Several leads are calculated from combinations of the same electrode voltages.","B":"The limb electrodes are relocated to the chest during every cardiac cycle.","C":"Two extra electrodes inside the heart are automatically activated for recording.","D":"Each electrode produces one lead, with two more produced by blood-pressure sensors."},"answer":"A","why":"A lead is an electrical viewpoint calculated from one or more electrode potentials, so ten physical contacts can generate twelve different standard recordings.","not":{"B":"The limb contacts remain in place during a standard recording.","C":"Routine twelve-lead ECG does not require intracardiac electrodes.","D":"All twelve are electrical leads; pressure sensors do not generate extra leads."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:b3bbd820","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"Standard Leads II and III both use the left leg as their positive electrode. What differentiates their negative electrodes?","options":{"A":"Lead II uses the left arm; Lead III uses the right arm.","B":"Lead II uses the right leg; Lead III uses the left arm.","C":"Lead II uses the right arm; Lead III uses the left arm.","D":"Lead II uses the left leg; Lead III uses the right leg."},"answer":"C","why":"In the Einthoven limb-lead system, Lead II runs from right arm negative to left leg positive; Lead III runs from left arm negative to left leg positive.","not":{"A":"This reverses the actual negative-electrode assignments of the two leads.","B":"The right-leg contact is used for electrical noise reduction.","D":"The left leg is the positive, not negative, terminal in both leads."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:12181fdb","chapter":"ecg","section":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","type":"recall","stem":"Which augmented limb lead views the heart from the left arm as its positive pole?","options":{"A":"Augmented lead aVR.","B":"Augmented lead aVL.","C":"Augmented lead aVF.","D":"Standard limb Lead I."},"answer":"B","why":"The letters of the augmented leads denote the positive viewing electrode: aVR is right arm, aVL is left arm and aVF is left foot.","not":{"A":"aVR points towards the right arm, not the left arm.","C":"aVF points towards the left foot, not the left arm.","D":"Lead III is a bipolar standard limb lead, not an augmented lead."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:02009354","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"mechanism","stem":"The SA node fires, and a P wave then appears. What produces the visible P-wave deflection?","options":{"A":"The opening of aortic and pulmonary valves.","B":"The electrical discharge of the tiny SA node alone.","C":"Depolarisation spreading across the atrial myocardium.","D":"Repolarisation spreading across the ventricular walls."},"answer":"C","why":"The SA node triggers atrial activation, but its own tissue is too small to produce the visible deflection; the P wave reflects depolarisation of atrial muscle.","not":{"A":"Valvular opening is a mechanical event not recorded as a P wave.","B":"The isolated SA-node signal is too small to appear as the surface P wave.","D":"Ventricular repolarisation is represented mainly by the T wave."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:2d5d20c0","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"calculation","stem":"At standard paper speed, a P wave spans 2.5 small squares. How does its duration compare with the stated normal range?","options":{"A":"0.04 s, shorter than the expected P-wave duration.","B":"0.10 s, within the expected P-wave duration.","C":"0.20 s, longer than the expected P-wave duration.","D":"0.25 s, longer than the expected P-wave duration."},"answer":"B","why":"At 25 mm/s a small square equals 0.04 s. Thus 2.5 × 0.04 = 0.10 s, at the upper end of the stated 0.08–0.10 s range.","not":{"A":"The calculation uses only one small square rather than 2.5 squares.","C":"This assigns 0.08 s to each small square, twice its actual time.","D":"This incorrectly assigns 0.10 s to each small square."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:1b9db59e","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"An ECG marker is placed at the beginning of the P wave. Where must the second marker go to measure the PR interval?","options":{"A":"At the beginning of the QRS complex.","B":"At the end of the ventricular QRS complex.","C":"At the beginning of the T wave.","D":"At the end of the ventricular T wave."},"answer":"A","why":"The PR interval measures from the onset of atrial depolarisation (P-wave onset) to the onset of ventricular depolarisation (QRS onset).","not":{"B":"Including all of QRS would extend beyond the PR interval.","C":"The start of T occurs after ventricular depolarisation.","D":"The end of T is used to mark the end of the QT interval."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:b8e4adbe","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"mechanism","stem":"A student calls the entire PR interval “the AV nodal delay.” What important part of the interval does this description miss?","options":{"A":"The time between the end of QRS and the start of T.","B":"The period during which the ventricular myocardium is repolarising.","C":"The delay between ventricular systole and valve opening.","D":"The spread through atria and conduction towards the ventricles."},"answer":"D","why":"PR includes the P wave itself and conduction through atrial tissue, AV node and His–Purkinje system before QRS begins. AV nodal delay is only one component.","not":{"A":"End-QRS to start-T corresponds to the ST segment.","B":"Ventricular repolarisation is represented by T and included in QT.","C":"Mechanical valve events are not boundaries of the PR interval."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:f7259e3f","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"A trace has a clear P wave followed by a flat stretch before QRS. Which measurement excludes the P wave itself?","options":{"A":"The PR interval.","B":"The QT interval.","C":"The PR segment.","D":"The QRS duration."},"answer":"C","why":"The PR segment extends from the end of the P wave to the start of QRS. In contrast, the PR interval begins at P-wave onset.","not":{"A":"The PR interval explicitly contains the P wave.","B":"QT starts with QRS and continues through the T wave.","D":"QRS duration covers ventricular depolarisation rather than the pre-QRS stretch."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:bc52a5fe","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why is the QRS complex generally larger than the P wave in a normal surface ECG?","options":{"A":"Ventricular cells depolarise only after atrial cells repolarise.","B":"Atrial muscle produces a larger combined electrical potential.","C":"The ventricular activation lasts substantially longer than atrial activation.","D":"The ventricular myocardium has much more electrically active muscle mass."},"answer":"D","why":"The ventricles contain far more working myocardium than the atria, so their depolarisation usually produces a larger summed surface potential.","not":{"A":"The timing of atrial repolarisation does not explain the normal amplitude difference.","B":"The atria have much less muscle mass than the ventricles.","C":"A longer duration does not by itself explain greater voltage amplitude."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:fbde833c","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"calculation","stem":"A QRS complex occupies two small horizontal squares at 25 mm/s. What is the best interpretation?","options":{"A":"It lasts 0.08 s, within the stated normal range.","B":"It lasts 0.16 s, above the stated usual normal range.","C":"It lasts 0.20 s, above the stated normal range.","D":"It lasts 0.40 s, above the stated normal range."},"answer":"A","why":"Each small square represents 0.04 s; two squares represent 0.08 s, falling inside the slide’s normal QRS duration of 0.06–0.10 s.","not":{"B":"This doubles the proper time value for two small squares.","C":"This confuses two small squares with one large square.","D":"This assigns 0.20 s to each small square."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:577d4de2","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"recall","stem":"A ventricular complex first rises above the baseline and then deflects below it. How is the downward deflection after the R wave named?","options":{"A":"P wave.","B":"Q wave.","C":"T wave.","D":"S wave."},"answer":"D","why":"By convention, the R wave is the first upward QRS deflection and the S wave is the downward deflection occurring after R.","not":{"A":"The P wave represents atrial depolarisation before the QRS.","B":"A Q wave is a negative QRS deflection before the first R wave.","C":"The T wave represents ventricular repolarisation after QRS."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:c1a8895f","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why is a distinct atrial repolarisation wave usually absent from a normal ECG?","options":{"A":"The atria cannot repolarise until ventricular diastole begins.","B":"Atrial repolarisation generates no electrical voltage differences.","C":"Its small electrical signal is obscured by the ventricular QRS complex.","D":"Atrial repolarisation occurs just before the SA node starts the next beat."},"answer":"C","why":"Atrial repolarisation occurs around the time of ventricular depolarisation; the much larger QRS electrical signal masks it on the surface trace.","not":{"A":"Atrial repolarisation does not need to await the beginning of ventricular diastole.","B":"Repolarisation does generate voltage differences, although the signal is small.","D":"That statement does not explain why the wave is not separately visible."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:cba111f2","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"mechanism","stem":"The ST segment is nearly flat despite an actively contracting ventricle. What electrical state best explains that appearance?","options":{"A":"The ventricle has completed repolarisation and returns to electrical rest.","B":"Most ventricular cells are depolarised together in their plateau phase.","C":"The atria and ventricles are depolarising in opposite directions.","D":"The AV node is holding all impulses before ventricular activation."},"answer":"B","why":"With much of the ventricular myocardium at a similar depolarised membrane potential during the plateau, there is little net voltage difference to register.","not":{"A":"Ventricular repolarisation occurs during the following T wave, not before ST.","C":"Atrial and ventricular opposing depolarisations do not account for normal ST isoelectricity.","D":"By ST, the impulse has already activated the ventricles."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:b08bc03e","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"clinical-application","stem":"An ECG develops a new shift of the ST segment above or below the baseline. Which process specifically makes this segment clinically important?","options":{"A":"Myocardial ischaemia or inadequate oxygenation can alter ST level.","B":"Normal atrial depolarisation creates the ST deflection of each beat.","C":"A decrease in cardiac preload directly determines ST segment height.","D":"The onset of valve closure normally lifts the ST segment above baseline."},"answer":"A","why":"Ventricular ischaemia or hypoxia can disturb membrane potentials and cause ST elevation or depression, so the segment is useful in recognising myocardial injury patterns.","not":{"B":"Atrial depolarisation is the P wave and normally precedes ST.","C":"Preload is a filling variable and does not directly set ST height.","D":"ST displacement is an electrical finding, not the normal marker of valve closure."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:ecfcc713","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"Why is the normal T wave generally broader than the QRS complex?","options":{"A":"The T wave covers atrial and ventricular depolarisation together.","B":"Ventricular depolarisation spreads more slowly than repolarisation.","C":"The T wave corresponds to ventricular contraction rather than electricity.","D":"Ventricular repolarisation usually takes longer than its depolarisation."},"answer":"D","why":"The T wave reflects the slower process of ventricular repolarisation, while QRS represents rapid ventricular depolarisation through the conduction system.","not":{"A":"Atrial and ventricular depolarisation are represented mainly by P and QRS.","B":"The statement reverses the usual relative time courses.","C":"The T wave is still an electrical event, not the force of contraction."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:7355d468","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"recall","stem":"A small additional deflection appears after the T wave in an otherwise interpretable trace. Which description fits the U wave?","options":{"A":"Atrial depolarisation occurring before the next P wave.","B":"Rapid ventricular depolarisation immediately before QRS.","C":"A possible late ventricular repolarisation phenomenon.","D":"A ventricular plateau interval between QRS and T."},"answer":"C","why":"The lecture describes a U wave, when seen after T, as the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation. Its precise physiological origin is uncertain.","not":{"A":"Atrial depolarisation is the P wave preceding QRS.","B":"Rapid ventricular depolarisation is represented by QRS, not U.","D":"The ventricular plateau is associated with the ST segment."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:f7d1ebb2","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"An interval spans the first deflection of QRS through the end of the T wave. What is being measured?","options":{"A":"Only the ventricular plateau phase.","B":"Ventricular depolarisation plus repolarisation.","C":"Atrial depolarisation plus AV nodal conduction.","D":"Atrial repolarisation plus ventricular filling."},"answer":"B","why":"The QT interval begins at QRS onset and ends at T-wave completion, spanning ventricular electrical activation and recovery.","not":{"A":"The plateau is mostly reflected in ST, only part of the QT interval.","C":"Atrial activation through AV conduction is assessed by PR.","D":"QT does not directly measure atrial recovery or mechanical filling."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:28c3c080","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"mechanism","stem":"On consecutive recordings, the heart rate increases and the QT interval decreases. Which explanation best accounts for this change?","options":{"A":"The duration of ventricular electrical recovery generally shortens as rate rises.","B":"The amount of ventricular myocardium decreases as heart rate rises.","C":"The QRS complex converts into a P wave at faster heart rates.","D":"The electrical calibration becomes weaker during tachycardia, shortening the trace."},"answer":"A","why":"QT includes ventricular repolarisation as well as depolarisation, and its duration is rate-dependent; with increasing heart rate, ventricular electrical recovery typically shortens.","not":{"B":"The ventricular muscle mass does not change with each rise in rate.","C":"The identities of P and QRS waves do not switch with rate.","D":"Paper calibration is a machine setting, not a consequence of heart rate."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:f1df5d31","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"calculation","stem":"Using Bazett’s formula, QTc = QT/√RR, what is QTc when QT is 0.36 s and RR is 0.81 s?","options":{"A":"0.29 s, because QT is multiplied by RR.","B":"0.32 s, because QT is multiplied by √RR.","C":"0.44 s, because RR is added to QT first.","D":"0.40 s, because QT is divided by 0.90."},"answer":"D","why":"Since √0.81 = 0.90, Bazett’s correction is 0.36/0.90 = 0.40 s. The correction adjusts the observed QT for heart rate.","not":{"A":"Multiplying by RR is not Bazett’s rate-correction formula.","B":"QT should be divided by √RR, not multiplied by it.","C":"Adding QT and RR has no role in the stated equation."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:38adb379","chapter":"ecg","section":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"A corrected QT interval is 0.46 s. Relative to the upper limit shown in the ECG diagram, how should it be described?","options":{"A":"Below the displayed upper limit of 0.40 s.","B":"Exactly equal to the displayed upper limit of 0.46 s.","C":"Above the displayed upper limit of 0.44 s.","D":"Within the displayed upper limit of 0.50 s."},"answer":"C","why":"The annotated ECG slide gives a QTc upper limit of 0.44 s. A QTc of 0.46 s exceeds that displayed threshold by 0.02 s.","not":{"A":"The diagram uses 0.44 s for QTc; 0.40 s is part of its uncorrected QT range.","B":"The observed value of 0.46 s is not the stated threshold.","D":"The annotated diagram does not give 0.50 s as its QTc upper limit."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:7b773af4","chapter":"ecg","section":"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events","type":"mechanism","stem":"A pressure tracing shows atrial contraction shortly after an ECG P wave and ventricular contraction shortly after QRS. What principle explains both delays?","options":{"A":"Mechanical contraction normally finishes before electrical activation begins in the chamber.","B":"Electrical excitation normally precedes the mechanical response of cardiac muscle.","C":"Atrial and ventricular pressures directly create the corresponding ECG waves.","D":"The T wave must occur before either chamber can begin contraction."},"answer":"B","why":"The electrical depolarisation that activates cardiac muscle precedes its force development. P precedes atrial systole, and QRS precedes ventricular systole.","not":{"A":"This reverses the normal electrical-to-mechanical sequence.","C":"Pressure changes follow electrical activation and are not what skin ECG leads measure.","D":"The T wave reflects ventricular electrical recovery, after ventricular depolarisation."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:736d0433","chapter":"ecg","section":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","type":"calculation","stem":"An ECG machine runs at 25 mm/s. How much time passes while the paper advances by 1 mm horizontally?","options":{"A":"0.04 s.","B":"0.10 s.","C":"0.20 s.","D":"0.50 s."},"answer":"A","why":"At a speed of 25 mm each second, 1 mm represents 1/25 s = 0.04 s, the duration of one small horizontal grid square.","not":{"B":"0.10 s does not correspond to a 1-mm horizontal square at this speed.","C":"0.20 s is the time represented by one 5-mm large square.","D":"0.50 s would occupy 12.5 small squares."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:37f51db5","chapter":"ecg","section":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"At standard calibration, which pair correctly describes one large ECG square?","options":{"A":"0.04 s horizontally and 0.5 mV vertically.","B":"0.20 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically.","C":"0.20 s horizontally and 0.5 mV vertically.","D":"0.50 s horizontally and 0.2 mV vertically."},"answer":"C","why":"A large square is 5 mm wide and high. Time: 5 × 0.04 = 0.20 s; voltage: 5 × 0.1 = 0.5 mV.","not":{"A":"0.04 s applies to one small horizontal square, not a large one.","B":"0.1 mV applies to one small vertical square, not five.","D":"Neither dimension matches the 5-mm standard calibration."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:6bed1585","chapter":"ecg","section":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","type":"calculation","stem":"At 25 mm/s, a PR interval extends across 4.5 small squares. Which interpretation is most accurate?","options":{"A":"0.09 s, shorter than the stated normal PR-interval range.","B":"0.18 s, inside the stated PR-interval range.","C":"0.24 s, above the stated PR-interval range.","D":"0.90 s, above the stated PR-interval range."},"answer":"B","why":"The horizontal duration is 4.5 × 0.04 = 0.18 s. This lies within the 0.12–0.20 s interval given in the lecture.","not":{"A":"This incorrectly halves the duration obtained from 4.5 small squares.","C":"This is not the product of 4.5 and 0.04 s.","D":"This incorrectly assigns 0.20 s to each small square."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:3bf24ff8","chapter":"ecg","section":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","type":"calculation","stem":"The R wave rises 7 mm above the baseline at 10 mm/mV calibration. What is its recorded amplitude?","options":{"A":"0.07 mV.","B":"0.14 mV.","C":"1.40 mV.","D":"0.70 mV."},"answer":"D","why":"Standard vertical calibration is 10 mm per 1 mV, so each millimetre is 0.1 mV. A 7-mm height therefore equals 0.7 mV.","not":{"A":"This treats one millimetre as 0.01 mV instead of 0.1 mV.","B":"This divides 1 mV by 7 mm rather than converting the recorded height.","C":"This doubles the correct voltage for the 7-mm deflection."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:ccb84b75","chapter":"ecg","section":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","type":"data-interpretation","stem":"A measured QRS occupies 3.5 small squares at 25 mm/s. Which statement is supported by the lecture’s usual duration range?","options":{"A":"It lasts 0.14 s and is longer than the stated usual range.","B":"It lasts 0.10 s and is at the upper edge of the stated range.","C":"It lasts 0.07 s and is within the stated usual range.","D":"It lasts 0.35 s and is longer than the stated usual range."},"answer":"A","why":"At 25 mm/s, 3.5 small squares equal 3.5 × 0.04 = 0.14 s, above the lecture’s usual QRS duration of 0.06–0.10 s.","not":{"B":"0.10 s corresponds to 2.5 small squares at the standard speed.","C":"0.07 s incorrectly halves the 0.14-s measured duration.","D":"0.35 s does not follow from the standard 0.04-s small-square duration."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:f8aa44ef","chapter":"ecg","section":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","type":"application","stem":"A recording is repeated at 50 mm/s instead of 25 mm/s, without changing the heartbeat. How does the same 0.16-s PR interval appear?","options":{"A":"It occupies half as many horizontal squares as on the slower recording.","B":"It occupies twice the former number of horizontal squares.","C":"It occupies the same number of horizontal squares.","D":"It occupies twice the former number of vertical squares."},"answer":"B","why":"Doubling paper speed doubles the horizontal distance covered during a fixed physiological interval. A 0.16-s PR interval spans 8 mm at 50 mm/s versus 4 mm at 25 mm/s.","not":{"A":"A faster recording speed spreads the same time over more paper, not less.","C":"The number of squares changes because time calibration depends on paper speed.","D":"Vertical squares measure voltage and are unaffected by horizontal paper speed."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:0aef4034","chapter":"ecg","section":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","type":"calculation","stem":"A regular rhythm has 24 small squares between adjacent R waves at standard paper speed. Approximately what is the heart rate?","options":{"A":"50 beats/min.","B":"60 beats/min.","C":"63 beats/min.","D":"80 beats/min."},"answer":"C","why":"The rate in a regular rhythm is 1500 divided by the R–R separation in small squares: 1500/24 = 62.5 beats/min, approximately 63.","not":{"A":"50 beats/min corresponds to about 30 small squares between R waves.","B":"60 beats/min corresponds to about 25 small squares between R waves.","D":"80 beats/min corresponds to about 18.75 small squares between R waves."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:430a2050","chapter":"ecg","section":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","type":"calculation","stem":"The R–R separation is four large squares on a regular standard-speed ECG. What is the heart rate?","options":{"A":"60 beats/min.","B":"75 beats/min.","C":"90 beats/min.","D":"120 beats/min."},"answer":"B","why":"At 25 mm/s, a large square equals 0.20 s. Four large squares represent 0.8 s per beat, giving 60/0.8 = 75 beats/min.","not":{"A":"60 beats/min would require five large squares between R waves.","C":"90 beats/min would require about 3.33 large squares.","D":"120 beats/min would require 2.5 large squares."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:0a8a39f5","chapter":"ecg","section":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","type":"calculation","stem":"An irregular tracing contains eleven QRS complexes in a six-second span. What is the estimated average ventricular rate?","options":{"A":"66 beats/min.","B":"90 beats/min.","C":"100 beats/min.","D":"110 beats/min."},"answer":"D","why":"Six seconds is one tenth of a minute. Eleven QRS complexes over that span give an approximate average ventricular rate of 11 × 10 = 110 beats/min.","not":{"A":"This multiplies the QRS count by six, not by ten.","B":"A rate of 90 beats/min would correspond to nine complexes in six seconds.","C":"A rate of 100 beats/min would correspond to ten complexes in six seconds."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:daabca75","chapter":"ecg","section":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","type":"mechanism","stem":"Why is 1500 used to calculate rate from the number of small squares between R waves at 25 mm/s?","options":{"A":"It is the standard number of R waves recorded in one minute.","B":"It is the electrical voltage calibration in millivolts.","C":"It is the number of millimetres in one normal cardiac cycle.","D":"It is the number of 1-mm squares passing in one minute."},"answer":"D","why":"At 25 mm/s, the paper travels 25 × 60 = 1500 mm per minute. Dividing these 1500 small squares by squares per beat yields beats per minute.","not":{"A":"A person’s actual heart rate is not fixed at 1500 beats per minute.","B":"Vertical voltage is calibrated at 10 mm per mV.","C":"The distance travelled per cycle varies with RR duration."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:ffc323a0","chapter":"ecg","section":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","type":"conceptual-comparison","stem":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes first-degree AV block from second-degree AV block in the chapter’s basic ECG classification?","options":{"A":"Both have identical PR intervals on each beat.","B":"Both show independent P-wave and QRS rhythms.","C":"First-degree conducts every P; second-degree drops some.","D":"First-degree lacks P waves; second-degree lacks QRS complexes."},"answer":"C","why":"First-degree block delays conduction but retains a QRS after every P wave. In second-degree block, at least some atrial impulses fail to produce QRS complexes.","not":{"A":"Second-degree block is defined by intermittent failed conduction, not identical PR intervals.","B":"Independent atrial and ventricular rhythms indicate complete third-degree block.","D":"Both conditions can show P waves and QRS complexes on the trace."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:40cb02e6","chapter":"ecg","section":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","type":"clinical-application","stem":"A rhythm strip shows P waves at a steady rate and QRS complexes at another steady rate, with no consistent relationship. What is the best classification?","options":{"A":"Sinus rhythm with a normal PR interval.","B":"First-degree atrioventricular block.","C":"Second-degree atrioventricular block.","D":"Third-degree atrioventricular block."},"answer":"D","why":"Complete (third-degree) block produces atrioventricular dissociation: atria and ventricles follow independent rhythms without consistent P-to-QRS conduction.","not":{"A":"Normal sinus rhythm has a consistent P wave preceding each conducted QRS.","B":"First-degree block maintains a consistent P-to-QRS relationship with prolonged PR.","C":"Second-degree block has intermittent failure, rather than complete independence."}},{"id":"lt-lecture-7:c29c9089","chapter":"ecg","section":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","type":"clinical-application","stem":"Which recording satisfies the basic chapter criteria for a normal sinus rhythm at rest?","options":{"A":"Rate 72/min, equal R–R gaps, identical preceding P waves, PR 0.16 s.","B":"Rate 72/min, equal R–R gaps, no visible P waves, PR not measurable.","C":"Rate 72/min, unequal R–R gaps, identical preceding P waves, PR 0.16 s.","D":"Rate 72/min, equal R–R gaps, identical preceding P waves, PR 0.26 s."},"answer":"A","why":"Normal sinus rhythm at rest requires an appropriate rate, regular R–R intervals, a consistent P before each QRS, and normal conduction intervals; the listed 0.16-s PR satisfies this.","not":{"B":"Without a visible P wave before each QRS, sinus origin is not established.","C":"Unequal R–R intervals do not satisfy the chapter’s basic regularity criterion.","D":"A PR of 0.26 s exceeds the chapter’s stated 0.12–0.20-s range."}}]};
